BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Surviving and Living Fully: The River’s Response to AIDS
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261205T110000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261205T180000
UID:261118RysyF-2O80Gnk3TUkelyKA
DESCRIPTION:The Russian River Historical Society invites the community to 
 join *Surviving and Living Fully: The River’s Response to AIDS*\, a free
  day of history\, remembrance\, and celebration on Saturday\, December 5th
 \, at Dawn Ranch (the original Fife’s) in Guerneville. Timed to World AI
 DS Day\, the event will honor the Russian River community’s extraordinar
 y response to the HIV/AIDS epidemic during the 1980s and 1990s.\n\nAs HIV/
 AIDS devastated LGBTQ+ communities across the country\, something remarkab
 le happened along the Russian River. Gay\, lesbian\, and straight neighbor
 s came together to care for one another\, building networks of healthcare\
 , food delivery\, hospice care\, and community support. Their collective r
 esponse became an important example of how rural communities can unite in 
 the face of a crisis.\n\nFor the past two years\, the Russian River Histor
 ical Society has worked to document and preserve this largely untold chapt
 er of local history through a California State Library grant. The project 
 has created a digital archive of historical records from West County Healt
 h Centers and recorded and transcribed more than 30 oral histories and int
 erviews with community members and healthcare providers. The interviews in
 clude recordings from 2003\, 2005\, and 2026. These materials will be made
  publicly accessible through a partnership with Sonoma County Library.\n\n
 “Surviving and Living Fully” will bring this history to life through w
 alking tours of historic Gay Guerneville\, a special presentation featurin
 g voices from the oral history archive\, and opportunities for community m
 embers to share their own memories. The presentation will revisit the chal
 lenges of the epidemic while highlighting the compassion\, resilience\, an
 d collective action that sustained the community.\n\nThe day will begin wi
 th small-group walking tours departing from the Guerneville Safeway parkin
 g lot at 11 a.m.\, noon\, and 1 p.m. The main presentation will take place
  from 2–4 p.m.\, followed by a hosted reception and dance from 4–6 p.m
 . featuring a disco-inspired DJ set by DJ Deks.\n\nThroughout the day\, at
 tendees can visit exhibits and community tables hosted by Face to Face\, F
 ood for Thought\, West County Health Centers\, Positive Images\, Sonoma Co
 unty Library’s Here & Queer Archive\, and the Russian River Historical S
 ociety. Archival photographs\, the original ACT UP banner\, and historical
  records from the Russian River Health Center will also be on display. A r
 emembrance table will offer space to honor loved ones lost to AIDS and sha
 re their stories.\n\n“This project is about preserving the stories of th
 e people who showed up for one another during an incredibly difficult time
 \,” said the project team. “We hope this gathering will honor those we
 've lost\, celebrate the community that came together\, and connect these 
 stories with younger generations.”\n\nThe event is free and open to the 
 entire community\, thanks to generous support from the California State Li
 brary and community partners. Registration is required. Walking tours requ
 ire advance registration\, and volunteer opportunities are available.\n\nT
 o volunteer or learn more\, visit **russianriverhistory.org** or email **l
 gbtq@russianriverhistory.org**.\n\n**About the Russian River Historical So
 ciety:** The Russian River Historical Society collects\, preserves\, and p
 resents materials related to the history of the Russian River area and its
  people.\n\n**About the Project:** The project was made possible through t
 he California State Library’s Preservation & Accessibility of California
 ’s LGBTQ+ History grant program\, with support from Sonoma County Librar
 y\, West County Health Centers\, and community volunteers. The resulting d
 igital archive and oral histories will help ensure this important history 
 remains accessible to future generations.
LOCATION:Dawn Ranch 
URL:http://bit.ly/aids-response
X-SOURCE:North Bay Bohemian
END:VEVENT
